.TH std::ranges::subrange::next 3 "2026.11.10" "http://cppreference.com" "C++ Standard Libary"
.SH NAME
std::ranges::subrange::next \- std::ranges::subrange::next

.SH Synopsis
constexpr subrange next( std::iter_difference_t<I> n = 1 ) const& \fB(1)\fP \fI(since C++20)\fP
requires std::forward_iterator<I>;
constexpr subrange next( std::iter_difference_t<I> n = 1 ) &&;    \fB(2)\fP \fI(since C++20)\fP

Returns a subrange whose begin_ is incremented (or decremented if n is negative). The
actual increment (or decrement) operation is performed by advance().

1) Returns a copy of *this.
Equivalent to: auto tmp = *this;
tmp.advance(n);
return tmp;.
2) Returns a subrange moved from *this.
Equivalent to: advance(n);
return std::move(*this);.

  Parameter

n - number of maximal increments of the iterator

.SH Return value

As described above.

.SH Notes

The difference between this function and advance() is that the latter performs the
increment (or decrement) in place.

.SH Example


// Run this code

#include <array>
#include <iterator>
#include <print>
#include <ranges>

int main()
{
    std::array arr{1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7};
    std::ranges::subrange sub{std::next(arr.begin(), 2), std::prev(arr.end(), 2)};
    std::println("1) sub: {}", sub);
    std::println("2) sub: {}", sub.next());
    std::println("3) sub: {}", sub.next(2));
}

.SH Output:

1) sub: [3, 4, 5]
2) sub: [4, 5]
3) sub: [5]

